   105	## 1.2 The verifier: one acceptance path per layer
   106	
   107	Carried in the order the families' models perform them. Step 1 is first
   108	because A3 §A3.2's chain begins with accepted external evidence
   109	(`docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-3.md:110-120`). The standing path runs
   110	**parallel to**, never sequenced behind, the envelope path: S-STANDING's
   111	SS.Q3 prediction records that sequencing them shadows the missing check
   112	and makes a companion unable to fail (`s-standing/PREDICTIONS.md:385-392`).
   113	
   114	| Order | Check | Family transcribed |
   115	|---|---|---|
   116	| 1 | Accepted authority evidence, `checksign(ev, kCh) = (STMT_DIGEST, h(t))` (D-1) | S-P1 / first-link spike |
   117	| 2 | Key binding: the frame's `kfp` is `fp(kX)`, and `mh = h(t)` | S-P3 |
   118	| 3 | Set logic: the signed `t`'s required set names the slot, `fp(kX) = kfpr`, every required slot satisfied | S-P2 |
   119	| 4 | Common content: `ota = otb`, `cva = cvb`, `pla = plb`, plus the three fields pinned through `t` | S-P2 Q6 |
   120	| 5 | Type, opacity and recorded-version checks, and `InnerCheck` for `lyr ≠ L0` | S-P7 |
   121	| 6 | Possession over the **accepted manifest** under the **accepting key**, `checksign(ppf, kX) = (POSS, t)` (D-3) | S-P3 / S-P2 |
   122	| 7 | The standing path: lineage lookup on `h(attemptCore(...))`, terminal disposition, entitled-key comparison against `t`'s `kfpr`; and the **`TLR` tag check** on the lineage record's signature, `let (=TLR, …) = checksign(tlrSig, kT)` (D-6) — the check **C-C18** unbinds *(named here by the AMENDMENT of 2026-09-18)* | S-STANDING |
   123	
   124	## 1.3 The `LayerAccepted` event, exactly as LEDGER §6 fixes it
   125	
   126	`LEDGER.md:1315`, with correction (c) at `:2249-2273`:
   127	
   128	```
   129	event LayerAccepted(bitstring, bitstring, bitstring, bitstring,
   130	                    bitstring, pkey, bitstring, bitstring, bitstring).
   131	(* aid, lyr, t, ev, slot, kX, ppf, sg, fb *)
   132	```
   133	
   134	`aid` is **a fresh `new aid: bitstring`, minted once per presentation
   135	AT INTAKE, and nothing else**: the aggregate package term does **not**
   136	serve, because replaying one package gives distinct executions the same
   137	term. *(REPAIR 2026-09-17, Codex round 2 finding 1. The withdrawn
   138	wording, quoted: “**a fresh `new aid: bitstring`, minted once per
   139	completed acceptance, and nothing else**”. It placed the mint **after**
   140	the checks, which is what let C-Q6's (E1) read the shared name as a
   141	certificate that the envelope path had **succeeded**. Nothing else about
   142	`aid` changes.)*
   143	
   144	**Where `aid` is minted** *(REPAIR 2026-09-17, Codex round 2 finding 1)*.
   145	The fresh name is minted **at intake**, by a **front-end process that
   146	reads the presented bundle once** and forwards it on **private channels
   147	to the envelope path and the standing path in parallel** — §1.2's
   148	“parallel to, never sequenced behind” and encoding rule (1) of §2.
   149	Both `LayerAccepted` **and** `EstablishedWrapped` (§3, C-Q6 (1)) carry
   150	**that** `aid`. **The ledger's carried-forward correction is preserved
   151	and strengthened, not relaxed** (`LEDGER.md:2249-2273`): **one fresh name
   152	per acceptance, shared across its slots, followed by
   153	`AcceptanceComplete`** — and the name still cannot be duplicated by
   154	replay, because it is `new` per **presentation** rather than derived
   155	from the package term. What changes is only that **the name exists
   156	before any check runs**, so a path may carry it without thereby
   157	asserting that the other path succeeded.
   158	
   159	**THE PER-ACCEPTANCE NAME IS THE PAIR `(aid, lyr)`, NOT `aid` ALONE**
   160	*(REPAIR 2026-09-17, Codex round 3 finding 1)*. *The withdrawn wording,
   161	quoted: “a bundle still yields **at most one** acceptance”.* That is
   162	**wrong for a wrapped bundle**. A wrapped presentation is accepted once
   163	**per layer**: each layer's acceptance closes with its own
   164	`AcceptanceComplete(aid, lyr, t)`, so one bundle yields **one
   165	acceptance per layer** and up to three (`L0`, `L1`, `L2`) at depth 2.
   166	Since the round-2 repair moved the mint to **intake**, all of them
   167	share the **one** `aid`, and §1.1's same-key wrapping with the
   168	one-key/one-manifest assignment lets them share `t` as well. The unit
   169	the ledger's carried-forward correction names — *one fresh name per
   170	acceptance, shared across its slots* — is therefore the **pair
   171	`(aid, lyr)`**: `aid` fresh per **presentation** (no replay can
   172	duplicate it), `lyr` distinguishing the acceptances **within** one
   173	presentation. Every construction in this file that means *"this
   174	acceptance"* — link 2b's grouping, its permitted projection (§3, link
   175	2b), and C-Q6's join — is read against the **pair**. **Shown:**
   176	`cap9_sp2_slots_projection_no_lyr.out` — two layers sharing one intake
   177	`aid` and one `t`, slot B reported at one layer only — leaves the
   178	`aid`-only projection **`is true`** (`:1239`) while the layer-indexed
   179	form is **`is false`** (`:1454`), with the witnesses reachable (`:675`,
   180	`:913`, `:1162`). Nothing else about `aid` changes, and §1.3's
   181	signature is unchanged: `lyr` was always an argument of both events.
   182	
   183	`lyr` is the layer index (`L0` / `L1` / `L2`, depth bounded at 2);
   184	`t` the accepted authority tuple `authTuple(id, kfpr, sset, alg, ver)`
   185	(D-2); `ev` **the accepted evidence term** this acceptance consumed — **in strict, the PAIR of such terms, one per channel** *(REPAIR 2026-09-17, Codex round 7 finding 2; the paragraph below)*;
   186	`slot` the signer-slot identifier, `kfpr` as the signed set names it; `kX`
   187	the accepting key; `ppf` the accepted possession proof; `sg` the accepted
   188	signature term; `fb` the **presented** framed bytes. Emitted **once per
   189	signer slot**, all slots of one acceptance carrying the **same `aid`**,
   190	the report output in parallel with its continuation. **The standing
   191	path's `EstablishedWrapped` carries the same intake `aid` and is emitted
   192	by that path on its own, without waiting for `LayerAccepted`** (§3,
   193	C-Q6 (2), (E1) as repaired 2026-09-17).
   194	
   195	**`ev` IN STRICT IS THE PAIR** *(REPAIR 2026-09-17, Codex round 7 finding 2)*.
   196	The strict fixture has **two** authority channels and the verifier reads an
   197	evidence term on **each**, so "the accepted evidence term" is ambiguous
   198	exactly where C-C10's isolation control lives: with the compromised
   199	channel's check deleted, the verifier checks one term and the acceptance
   200	carries the other unexamined. **In strict, `ev` in `LayerAccepted` is the
   201	pair of evidence terms the acceptance carried, one per channel; in
   202	degraded it is the sole term.** *The withdrawn phrase, quoted: "`ev` **the
   203	accepted evidence term** this acceptance consumed".* Its sense is not
   204	withdrawn — this is that phrase **made exact for two channels** — and
   205	**`LayerAccepted`'s signature is not changed**: `ev` remains one argument,
   206	whose term in strict is a pair. **This is a CONTESTABLE contract term**, a
   207	clerk disposition of the owner instance, open to the author at the freeze
   208	commit: the alternative — observing only the **retained, checked** term —
   209	is a second contract choice that does not follow from deleting the
   210	opposite check, and it would leave C-C10's isolation control all green.
   211	The reading registered here is the one under which **deleting either
   212	check reds link 1a**, the right property for a binding link. **Shown, on
   213	copies of a committed family model:** the channel-specific link-1a judge
   214	is **green** on the strict baseline
   215	(`cap20d_sp1_strict_base_evidence.out:589`) and **red** on both deletions
   216	(`cap20f_sp1_strict_cc10_drop_compromised_evidence.out:737`,
   217	`cap20g_sp1_strict_cc10_drop_honest_evidence.out:1055`). No capstone model
   218	exists and none of these is a capstone result.
   219	
   220	**THE REPORT CONTRACT UNDER COMPANIONS** *(REPAIR 2026-09-17, Codex
   221	round 9 finding 1 — **this file's contract term, CONTESTABLE by the
   222	author at the freeze commit; the FOURTH such choice**, beside same-key
   223	wrapping, the compromised-channel-only reading of "adversary-enrolled",
   224	and `ev` as the pair in strict)*. **Under every companion,
   225	`LayerAccepted` and every judge report at a layer carry the terms THAT
   226	LAYER'S ACCEPTANCE WAS ABOUT** — the **presented** `(t, ppf, sg, fb)` of
   227	that layer, under the key the (mutated) verifier verified against — and
   228	**never** terms checked at **another** layer. **This is already what the
   229	immutable join requires** (`t = tI`, `ppf = ppfI`; §3, C-Q6 (3), the
   230	term-equality column, which is immutable and which no companion
   231	removes). It is **stated** here because it was left unspecified, and an
   232	unspecified report is a **builder choice that changes a result**: a
   233	builder could make a companion's failure invisible by reporting **what
   234	the verifier did check** in place of what the acceptance was about.
   235	**Shown, on copies of a committed family model.** C-C15(i)'s strict
   236	transcription checks the **outer** possession proof and then verifies the
   237	inner signature under `kW`; it never checks the presented inner `tI` or
   238	`ppfI`. With the C-Q9 structural possession judge added and **nothing
   239	else changed**, the inner acceptance reporting the **presented inner**
   240	`(kW, tI, ppfI)` makes C-Q9 **red** in both channel variants
   241	(`cap22e_sp7_strict_q6b_q9_inner.out:1334`;
   242	`cap22f_sp7_strict_q6b_q9_inner_repo.out:1334`) while the four S-P7
   243	queries stay green (`cap22e:569`, `:578`, `:587`, `:596`) and both
   244	witnesses stay reachable (`:871`, `:1075`); reporting the **checked
   245	outer** `(kW, tW, ppfW)` instead makes the same judge **green**
   246	(`cap22g_sp7_strict_q6b_q9_outer.out:1084`) — **the rejected reading**;
   247	and the **unchanged strict baseline**, whose acceptance reports its own
   248	**checked** inner terms, is green
   249	(`cap22d_sp7_strict_base_q9.out:1186`). **Why it is contestable, in one
   250	line:** the alternative — report the terms the verifier actually
   251	verified, whatever layer they came from — is a second contract choice
   252	that does not follow from any mutation, and under it C-C15(i)'s C-Q9
   253	stays green, so the two readings give **opposite colours on the same
   254	verifier**. The reading registered here is the one under which a
   255	companion that **stops checking** a term cannot hide that by
   256	**reporting** a different one. **`LayerAccepted`'s signature is
   257	unchanged** — this fixes what its existing arguments **denote** under a
   258	mutation, exactly as the `ev`-pair paragraph above does — and **no
   259	query, no companion, no probability and no timebox moves.** No capstone
   260	model exists and none of these `.out` files is a capstone result.
   261	
   262	| Event | Arguments | Emitted |
   263	|---|---|---|
   264	| `AuthorityEvidence` | `t`, `ev`, the channel key `ev` verified under | after the evidence check, before anything else is read (`:1314`) |
   265	| `ScopeReported` *(DECLARED HERE — REPAIR 2026-09-17, Codex round 6 finding 1)* | `aid`, `lyr`, `kA`, `idA` — the **envelope path's own attribution report**: the key and the issuer identity that path attributes the acceptance to | on the `scopeCh` report, **in parallel with the continuation** (encoding rule (1), §2), at every layer. **It is not a new check and not a new extraction**: it is the `scopeCh` report S-P7's models already emit — the correct model's at `sp7_q2_degraded_compromised.pv:303` (`out(scopeCh, (ctx, kI, idI, fbI))`), read by `ScopeJudge` at `:351` — carried here with §1.3's per-acceptance pair `(aid, lyr)` in place of the family's `ctx`. It is declared here because **link 6b now observes it** (§3, link 6b, as repaired for round 6 finding 1) and because **C-Q6's conjunct 3 consumes it** (§3, C-Q6 (5), where it was already named at `:1146-1151` but not declared beside `LayerAccepted`) |
   266	| `AcceptanceComplete` | `aid`, `lyr`, `t` | at the **single** acceptance point of that layer, after every `LayerAccepted` of that acceptance (`:1316`) |
   267	| `ChainBroken` | `lyr`, the failed link's name | by the judge; the subject of links 1a, 2a, 3, 4, 5, 6a, 6b **only**. Links 1b and 2b are correspondences (`:1317`, `:2213-2247`) |
   268	| `HonestChain` | `lyr`, `t` | the honest-flow witness; **must be reachable** (C-N1, `:1318`) |
