    58	
    59	## 0. What was built, and how the other files are derived
    60	
    61	`capstone.pv` is **the** composed model, in case **(a)** — strict, DNS channel compromised. It carries
    62	the §1.1 fixture, the §1.2 acceptance path per layer, the §1.3 events, and **every** registered query
    63	of that case in one file. The other cases and every companion are **derived from it** by
    64	`formal/suite/capstone/proverif/derive.py`, which applies named, exact-string edits inside named
    65	blocks of the model and writes a header into each generated file naming its case, what it mutates,
    66	which registered prediction it serves and the registered box. Nothing in `derive.py` edits
    67	`capstone.pv`. The delta for any generated file is therefore readable twice: in `derive.py`'s rule
    68	list, and as `diff capstone.pv <file>`.
    69	
    70	- **(a)** strict, `skD` public — `capstone.pv` itself (no copy: the file the registration names is the
    71	  file that runs, so the blob hash is its own).
    72	- **(b)** strict, `skR` public — `capstone_b.pv`, one line.
    73	- **(c)** strict, **both** channel keys public — `cap_cc01_c.pv`, one line. Per §1.1's cases table
    74	  this case **is** companion **C-C1**, not a correct-form run.
    75	- **(d)** degraded, sole channel public — `capstone_d.pv`, a **documented copy**: `derive.py`'s
    76	  `degraded()` transformation and nothing else (one authority channel; one evidence check; `ev` the
    77	  sole term rather than §1.3's pair; `AcceptS` in place of the library's `Accept`; **C-Q1 link 1b and
    78	  C-Q2 not asserted**, per §1.1's cases table and §3; **C-Q6 conjunct 1's unrestricted form not
    79	  asserted**, per §3 §(5)).
    80	
    81	`run_ladder.sh` runs the ladder in the registered order (§3), with one departure that is stated
    82	rather than taken silently. The registered order is *"C-Q1's links before every companion; (a), (b),
    83	(d) before (c); C-Q7, C-Q8, C-Q9 before links 3 and 4 and before C-Q6"* (`:301-304`). The last clause
    84	is a **query** order and is honoured by the declaration order inside `capstone.pv`. The first is a
    85	**run** order and is honoured: the case-(a) and case-(b) correct-form runs are a barrier and complete
    86	before any companion starts. **The departure:** cases (c) and (d) are materially more expensive than the
    87	strict correct-form cases — by a factor not yet measured under the repaired model (finding F-3) — so
    88	they run in their own slots **alongside** the strict companion waves rather than in front of them, and the case-(c) and
    89	case-(d) companions wait for them. What "(a), (b), (d) before (c)" protects — that no companion's
    90	colour is read before the correct model's is — is preserved for every case: no companion of a case is
    91	run before that case's correct-form run has completed. What is not preserved is that (d) finishes
    92	before (c) starts; they run concurrently, in separate processes with separate boxes. This is a
    93	scheduling choice and changes no colour. *(Dated note 2026-10-01: that describes ladder 1. Ladder 2 ran
    94	(a) and (b), then (d)'s correct form, then the companion waves of (a), (b) and (d), and case (c) alone
    95	last among the correct-form and C-C1 runs. It ran on 2026-09-28 after the host crash, and the eighteen
    96	remaining degraded companions followed it (`ladder.log:4-7`, `:70`, `:71`). No companion of any case
    97	started before that case's correct-form run had completed.)* `dev/` holds the development scratch, **none of it a result**:
    98	
    99	- `dev/first.out` — the first development run, made **before** the ladder and under the **superseded**
   100	  blob `508b9018…`. It is **not** byte-identical to today's `capstone.out` and must not be compared
   101	  with it; it is retained only as the record of the model's first clean pass.
   102	- `dev/cap_cc03_a_BROKEN_FIXTURE.out` — the quarantined run of **BF-1**, kept so the diagnosis can be
   103	  checked against its own output. Also under the superseded blob.
   104	- `dev/summarize.py`, `dev/mkresults.py`, `dev/assemble.py` — transcription helpers that turn the
   105	  `.out` files into the tables below and write them into this file. They decide nothing.
   106	- `dev/ladder.stdout` — the stdout of the superseded launches. The live ladder's is
   107	  `proverif/ladder.launch.log`.
   108	- *(Added 2026-10-01.)* `dev/assemble_l2.py` — the ladder-2 assembler. It keys every `RESULT` line by
   109	  its normalised text and stops on any line it cannot key exactly once — the ladder-1 helper silently
   110	  left six lines unkeyed in every C-C15 file, whose variables recut R-6 renumbers — and it carries the
   111	  per-companion, per-case registration read from §4 / §9's Cases cells, each with its line. It
   112	  compares; it decides nothing.
   113	- *(Added 2026-10-01.)* `dev/abl2a/` (the strict link-2b diagnosis behind recut R-9), `dev/recut_pre/`
   114	  (the pre-ladder full-file runs under the recut) and `dev/abl_d/` (the degraded-wave diagnosis). Scratch
   115	  runs under memory caps. **None of them is a result**, no cell is filled from them, and none is counted
   116	  in the ceiling.
   117	
   118	**TWO READINGS OF THE COMPANION BOX, AND WHICH ONE THIS FILE REPORTS UNDER.** The registration can
   119	be read two ways on how much wall clock a companion is allowed, and the cross-family reviewer will
   120	ask which governs, so both are recorded for every companion run and neither is decided here. *(REPAIR
   121	2026-10-01, skeptic S-4: the choice is now routed to the author, with its effect row by row; see section 3,
   122	*Which box reading each severing depends on*, and *ROUTED TO THE AUTHOR — second question*.)*
   123	
   124	- **The per-case-sum reading.** §3's timebox table declares a box per registered query and totals
   125	  them: *"**Total declared, per compromise case** | | **27** | **1185 min (19 h 45 m)**"*
   126	  (`:1595`), with the whole-ladder ceiling *"at most **4 × 1185 = 4740 min (79 h)**"* (`:1599`,
   127	  `:1620`). Each `.pv` here carries **every** registered query of its case, so on this reading a run
   128	  is allowed the sum of the boxes of the queries it carries — 1185 min for a strict case, 1095 min
   129	  for (d), which asserts neither link 1b nor C-Q2. **`run_ladder.sh` enforces this reading**, because
   130	  it is the one a single file carrying every query can be held to.
   131	- **The predicted-red reading.** §4's count paragraph says *"**No companion carries a timebox**:
   132	  §3's timebox table is over registered **queries** only, and **a companion runs inside the box of
   133	  the query it is predicted to red**"* (`:1867-1870`), with the per-companion assignments spelled out
   134	  where they are not obvious — *"C-C17 runs inside link 2a's 45-minute box"* and *"C-C16 runs inside
   135	  link 5's 60-minute box"* (`:1873-1875`), *"C-C18 runs inside C-Q10's 30-minute box"* (`:1875-1876`),
   136	  C-C8's isolation runs *"inside C-Q9's 60-minute box"* (§9(2)). On this reading a companion's box is
   137	  30 to 90 minutes, not 1185.
   138	
   139	Section 2 therefore carries **two columns**: `rc` and seconds under the per-case-sum box the ladder
   140	actually enforced, and whether that same run finished inside the box of the query it is predicted to
   141	red. Where a companion is predicted to red more than one query the **strictest** reading is taken —
   142	the **smallest** of their boxes — and the column names which query it is. **Nothing is re-run to
   143	produce this**: both columns are read off the one `ladder.log` line. If the strict reading governs,
   144	section 2's second column is the report under it; if the per-case-sum reading governs, the first
   145	column is.
   146	
   147	*(REPAIR 2026-10-01, skeptic S-10.)* Where the registration **assigns** a companion's box in words, section
   148	2's column does not use it: it takes the smallest box among the companion's predicted-red queries, 30
   149	minutes, for three companions the registration assigns elsewhere — C-C17 to link 2a's 45-minute box, C-C16 to
   150	link 5's 60-minute box, and C-C9 to link 2b's, which is 60 minutes (`PREDICTIONS.md:1873-1875`; §3's table,
   151	`:1588`). **No yes/no changes**, checked run by run: C-C9 ran 252, 255 and 1 377 s, inside both 30 and 60
   152	minutes; C-C16 ran 284 and 273 s in strict (inside both) and 6 671 s in (d) (outside both); C-C17 ran
   153	27 503 and 27 084 s (outside both). The generated column is therefore left as it is. Which reading governs
   154	at all is routed to the author (*ROUTED TO THE AUTHOR — second question*), and the row-by-row effect is in
   155	section 3 (*Which box reading each severing depends on*).
   156	
   157	**The whole-ladder ceiling.** §3 puts it at *"at most 4 × 1185 = 4740 min (79 h)"* (`:1620`). The
   158	ladder's total wall clock against that ceiling is recorded under **Not run** when it completes, and
   159	if it exceeds the ceiling this file says so plainly.
   160	
   161	**Timeboxes, and how they are read here.** §3's table declares a box **per registered query**, 27
   162	entries and 1185 min per compromise case. Each `.pv` here carries **every** registered query of its
   163	case, so each run is given the **per-case sum**: 1185 min for a strict case, 1095 min for (d), which
   164	asserts neither link 1b nor C-Q2. Because a whole-model run's wall clock is an upper bound on every
   165	query's own time, **a run that finishes under 30 minutes is inside the smallest registered box for
   166	every query it carries**; `ladder.log` records the seconds so that can be checked rather than taken
   167	on trust. No companion carries a box of its own (§4, "The count"); it runs inside the box of the
   168	query it is predicted to red, and a fortiori inside the sum.
