   913	(rows above).
   914	
   915	**The diagnosis, summarised** (`proverif/dev/abl_d/REPORT.md` §(a); scratch, one run at a time under a
   916	10 GiB cap, not a result). In (d) the sole authority key is public, so check 1 accepts any tuple the
   917	attacker mints. That includes a two-signer tuple whose slot-B fingerprint belongs to a `dsks`-derived
   918	key. Each failing companion of the first six deletes exactly the check that denied such a key a borrowed
   919	honest signature: the frame pin and `mh` (C-C2), the inner pin (C-C4), or the byte binding (C-C5, C-C6,
   920	C-C7(i)). C-C3, and C-C8 with it, open a second route through fingerprint-only honest possession. The
   921	report's words: *"The degraded delta removes the bound on tuples; the mutation removes the bound on keys.
   922	Either alone is finite; together they are not."* Measured in verbose traces, 85 % and 84 % of the
   923	saturated clauses carry a `dsks` term in `cc02_d` and `cc05_d`, against 1 % in `capstone_d`. Companions
   924	whose mutation reaches `WrapStage` stall on link 1a. Those confined to `AttestStage` pass links 1a and 2a
   925	and die on the first goal with a structured `authTuple` (link 2b). The report itself calls this *"an
   926	explanation fitted to these observations"*. **The five later failures fit it only in part.** C-C7(ii)
   927	and C-C8 stall on link 1a, as the split predicts. C-C8-i, C-C8-ii and C-C17 each mutate **both**
   928	`AttestStage` and `WrapStage`. By `diff capstone_d.pv cap_<c>_d.pv` the edits fall at `capstone_d.pv`
   929	lines 578-611 and 674-711 for C-C8-i, 580-618 and 676-718 for C-C8-ii, and 607 and 705 for C-C17,
   930	against `AttestStage` at `:564` and `WrapStage` at `:659`. Yet all three passed links 1a and 2a and died
   931	on link 2b's one-signer conjunct. C-C8-ii opens the honest-possession route that the diagnosis assigns
   932	to C-C3 and C-C8, and C-C8-i was diagnosed as opening *neither* route (§(a)). The diagnosis as written
   933	does not account for these three.
   934	
