    25	
    26	## (a) Diagnosis
    27	
    28	**Cause in one line:** the degraded setup lets the attacker vouch for any tuple; each failing companion removes the check that stopped a `dsks`-derived key from using a signature it didn't make.
    29	
    30	**The mechanism in full.**
    31	- In (d) the sole authority key `skS` is public (`capstone_d.pv:1069-1070`), so check 1 accepts any `authTuple` the attacker mints.
    32	- That includes a two-signer tuple whose slot-B fingerprint is that of a `dsks`-derived key (`tessera_theory.pvl:110-115`).
    33	- In the correct form such a key is still stopped. The only signature it verifies is somebody else's honest `sign((BYTES, frame), sk)`, whose frame names `fp(pk(sk))` and not the derived key. Two checks enforce this: the frame's `=fp(kX)` pin (`:608-611`) and the `=fbA` byte pin (`:619-620`).
    34	- Each failing companion deletes exactly the check that denied the `dsks` key that borrowed signature:
    35	  - C-C2: the frame pin and `mh`.
    36	  - C-C4: the inner pin.
    37	  - C-C5, C-C6, C-C7(i): the byte binding.
    38	- The product of attacker-minted tuples × the fixture's honest `BYTES` signatures × slots × layers then forms a clause family that saturation cannot close.
    39	- In strict (a)/(b) the second, uncompromised channel evidences only `M1`, `M2` and `MW`, so no tuple can name a `dsks` key at all.
    40	- **The degraded delta removes the bound on tuples; the mutation removes the bound on keys. Either alone is finite; together they are not.**
    41	
    42	**Verbose traces** (manual §6.7.2's procedure, 300 s each, `diag_summary.log`):
    43	
    44	| trace | file / query | clauses | with a `dsks` term | of those, on `signers1` | of those, slot B = `fp(pk(dsks…))` |
    45	|---|---|---|---|---|---|
    46	| D0 | cc02_d, 1a | 161,643 | 137,406 (85%) | 137,175 | 103,778 |
    47	| D3 | cc07i_d, 1a | 205,582 | 78,765 (38%) | 73,601 | 40,783 |
    48	| D4 | cc06_d, 1a | 110,410 | 80,705 (73%) | 77,369 | 37,806 |
    49	| D5 | cc05_d, conj 1 | 111,855 | 93,813 (84%) | 90,466 | 68,670 |
    50	| D1 | capstone_d, 1a (landed, 23 s) | 35,046 | 206 (1%) | 79 | 0 |
    51	| D2 | cc03_d, 1a | 196,504 | 214 (0%) | 83 | 0 |
    52	
    53	- The most-selected literal in D0 is `attacker(fp(pk(dsks(sign((BYTES,framed…))))))` (14,387 selections). Next come `attacker(fp(pk(k_1)))` and the honest `OT_WRAPPER` BYTES signatures (12,226).
    54	- `dsks` terms are never nested (maximum depth 1). The growth is in **width**: up to 38 / 59 / 47 distinct `dsks` terms in one clause (D0 / D3 / D4). This is a combinatorial family, not a loop.
    55	- **C-C3 is a second route that uses no `dsks` at all.** It makes possession fingerprint-only, so an honest key's one possession signature serves every tuple naming that key, and the `mh` guards are gone. In D2, 185,204 of 196,504 clauses (94%) carry an honest `sign((POSS, fp(pk(sk))), sk)`, and 160,455 carry an `OT_WRAPPER` frame. C-C8 uses the same route.
    56	
    57	**Why the depth-2 layer matters for C-C2 but not for C-C5.**
    58	- C-C2 mutates the two-signer branch of **both** `AttestStage` and `WrapStage`.
    59	- `X_cc02d_1a_wrapreverted` restores the pins in `WrapStage` only; `AttestStage` keeps the mutation. This is a diagnostic, not a registered ablation.
    60	- On link 1a:
    61	
    62	| run | what is present | outcome |
    63	|---|---|---|
    64	| X | mutation only in `AttestStage` | lands, 492 s / 1.13 GiB (abl.log:25, `.out:3505`) |
    65	| A1 | ablation step 1 | lands, 2418 s / 2.14 GiB (abl.log:20, `:4283`) |
    66	| A0 | mutation and depth-2 layer both present | times out, 3600 s / 6.71 GiB |
    67	
    68	- So the multiplier is the wrapper handing a `dsks`-accepted frame's inner blob down `stageCh` to the next layer (`capstone_d.pv:708-709`, `:767-768`).
    69	- C-C5's mutation is only in `AttestStage` (`cap_cc05_d.pv:596`, `:634-635`), which feeds no further layer. Dropping depth 2 changed nothing on its conj 1: B0 / B1 / B12 / B123 ran out of memory at 2354 / 2348 / 2774 / 2729 s. Deleting the two-signer branch lands it in 14 s, which fits D5 (96% of its `dsks` clauses are on `signers1`).
    70	
    71	**Why C-C4 and C-C5 pass link 1a but die on a 2b conjunct.** This is the same split.
    72	- Companions whose mutation reaches `WrapStage` **and** opens one of the two routes stall on link 1a: C-C2, C-C3, C-C6, C-C7(i), and C-C7(ii)/C-C8 among the interrupted runs.
    73	- C-C4 (`cap_cc04_d.pv:592`, `:622-623`) and C-C5 are confined to `AttestStage`. They close 1a and 2a, whose goal is a one-variable `ChainBroken` (dev/abl2a/REPORT.md:97-110). They then die on the first goal carrying a structured `authTuple`: conj 1 for C-C5, conj 2 for C-C4.
    74	- Exception: `cc08i_d` drops `mh` in both stages yet passed 1a, because it opens neither route.
    75	- This is an explanation fitted to these observations. Only C-C2's half was tested directly (by X).
