  1794	
  1795	**F-4 — the registration does not say what a WRAPPED TWO-SIGNER object looks like, and the capstone
  1796	is the first model where the question arises.** S-P7's embedding carries one inner frame and one
  1797	inner signature; S-P2's set logic gives a two-signer manifest two frames and two signatures; §1.1
  1798	composes both and §1.2 puts the set logic on every layer. The blob therefore had to widen (recut R-1).
  1799	This is an encoding consequence of the composition, and it is exactly the kind of thing the capstone
  1800	exists to surface: **no single-family model could have raised it.**
  1801	
  1802	**F-5 — §1.1's same-key wrapping forces the WRAPPER layer to be a two-signer acceptance, which §1.1
  1803	does not say.** The chain is: `skI` wraps its own attestation (§1.1's wrapper row); `skI`'s manifest
  1804	is the two-signer `M2` (§1.1's required-signer row); ONE KEY, ONE MANIFEST forbids `skI` a second
  1805	manifest; and §1.2's check 3 requires **every** required slot satisfied. So an `OT_WRAPPER` frame
  1806	under `skI` is accepted over `M2` and needs `skB2` to co-sign it. The fixture here therefore runs
  1807	`Wrapper(skB2, …)` beside `Wrapper(skI, …)`, and the honest wrapped witness is a two-slot acceptance
