  1819	the machine at 0 GB available and 5.4 of 8 GB swap; at 19:24, with only the two protected runs left,
  1820	it was at **0.2 GB available and 5.3 GB of swap** again, `cap_cc01_c` at **20.0-20.6 GB** and
  1821	`capstone_d` at **8.5-9.2 GB and rising**. **The two runs that were killed for it, with their numbers.** `cap_cc01_c`
  1822	(case (c)) reached **20.6 GB resident at 3 of 47 queries**, 44 minutes in, and was terminated for it;
  1823	`capstone_d` (case (d)) was measured climbing **12.7 → 14.7 GB in thirty seconds** while holding at
  1824	3 of 47. Neither was slow in the sense §3 prices; both were **large**. **What a future builder needs,
  1825	and should be given by amendment rather than rediscovered:** (1) a **memory box per run** beside the
  1826	time box, since a run that exceeds memory dies without an `rc` line unless something like
  1827	`mem_guard.sh` is watching; and (2) a concurrency rule of the form
  1828	**`JOBS = floor(RAM / largest observed run)`** — on this machine, with case (c) at 20.6 GB, that is
  1829	**1** for case (c) and 3 for a companion wave, not the 6 or 8 a core count suggests. **The
  1830	consequence for anyone re-running this ladder:** the concurrency that fits is set by 31 GB divided by
  1831	the largest run, not by the core count, and case (c) alone can approach the machine. A ceiling expressed only in minutes (4740) does not bound what this
  1832	model actually consumes, and a kernel OOM kill — unlike a timeout — produces no `rc` line, no
  1833	classification and no record at all. **This is a gap in the registration's mechanism-viability
  1834	apparatus, not a defect of the model**, and it is the single thing a future builder most needs to know
  1835	before choosing `JOBS`.
  1836	*(Dated note 2026-10-01, the facts since: on ubuntu24 the 80 GB per-process guard terminated **four
  1837	ladder-1 runs** and **four ladder-2 runs**. The seven ladder-2 timeouts peaked at 21.8-46.9 GiB. Memory
  1838	and the per-run box, not the query, decided every mechanism failure in both ladders. The ceiling these
  1839	costs produced is stated under* Not run, and the ceiling*: 559.7 % over both ladders, 556.8 % before the
  1840	three BF-4 re-runs.)*
  1841	
  1842	**F-7 — what a severed check costs falls on EVERY query, not on the query that sees it.** The
  1843	registration prices its timeout risk per query and per formula shape — link 2b's fresh-name
  1844	antecedent, C-Q6's lineage inside a wrapper. The observed driver is neither. **In the correct strict
  1845	model link 1a is resolved in the first seconds of a 374 s run; in a companion it is the query the run
  1846	sits on**: at about seven minutes of elapsed time `cap_cc07i_a` had not yet resolved link 1a and
  1847	`cap_cc02_a` had resolved 2 of its 47, though neither companion mutates anything link 1a observes.
  1848	What grows under a severed check is not that query but the reachable state space **every** query is
  1849	solved against. **This is a shape of cost the registration does not price anywhere**, and it is the
  1850	one a future builder most needs: the companion ladder's wall clock is set by *how many checks are
  1851	missing*, not by *which queries are registered red*. **No ablation is owed**: §3's ablation order
  1852	fires on a timeout, and no run has timed out — the boxes are 1185 min per strict case and 1095 for
  1853	(d). *(Corrected 2026-09-19: the withdrawn claim, quoted, rested on a mis-stated elapsed time —
  1854	"spends tens of minutes on its FIRST query" and "the companion ladder is days". Seven minutes is what
  1855	was observed; how long a companion takes is not yet measured.)* *(Dated note 2026-10-01: **"No ablation is
  1856	owed … no run has timed out" is superseded.** In ladder 1, C-C7(i) timed out in (a) and (b), and C-C7(ii)
  1857	and C-C14 were terminated by the guard; they were disposed of by a change of tactic (recut R-9), not by
  1858	ablation. In ladder 2, eleven degraded companions failed, and neither of §3's disposals disposes of them
  1859	(*The degraded wave*). The cost shape this finding describes is visible there: the failing degraded runs
  1860	stall on link 1a or link 2b, and neither query is in any failing companion's set (1).)*
  1861	
  1862	**F-6 — three registered sentences have no consumer in the registered query set.** §4's C-C7(ii)
  1863	sentence about a *honest-filtered* C-Q8, §9(1)'s C-C13(ii) per-layer assignment of link 6a's red, and
  1864	§9(4)'s C-C17 consequence on `PossessionTransplanted` each state an outcome for a relation that §3
  1865	does not register as a capstone query. They are recorded under **Blocked** (B-1, B-2, B-3) with the
  1866	smallest change that would close each. None of them is a defect of the model; each is a place where
  1867	the registration's prose ran ahead of its query list.
  1868	
  1869	**F-9 — the change of tactic moved no verdict, and it landed every strict run** *(added 2026-10-01)*.
  1870	Recut R-9 (`set selFun = Term.`) reproduces byte for byte every `RESULT` line of the 49 files that landed
  1871	under the default selection function (*Cross-check*). It turned every strict mechanism failure of ladder 1
  1872	into a landing: C-C7(i), C-C7(ii) and C-C14 in (a) and (b), and case (c). *(REPAIR 2026-10-01, skeptic S-8:
  1873	“and case (c)” is withdrawn as credit to the recut. In ladder 1 case (c) was not a mechanism failure: the
  1874	owner instance terminated it at 2 732 s on the 31 GB machine (`ladder1/ladder.log:24`), and it was never re-run
  1875	under ladder 1's model. Ladder 2 landed it in 4 279 s on ubuntu24, but there is no ladder-1 run on that
  1876	machine to compare, so nothing shows the recut was what it needed.)* The effect on cost is not uniform. Over
  1877	the 49 files that landed in both ladders, ladder 2's time ranges from 1/34 of ladder 1's (`cap_cc05_b`:
  1878	15 191 s to 442 s) to 1.5 times it (`capstone_d`: 4 257 s to 6 388 s). The correct forms and C-C10 got
  1879	slower, and the heaviest companions got much faster: `cap_cc05_a` took 17 934 s and 22.1 GiB in ladder 1
  1880	(`ladder1/ladder.log:78`) and 944 s and 0.8 GiB in ladder 2 (`ladder.log:12`). Part of ladder 1 ran on
  1881	wam-nuc and all of ladder 2 on ubuntu24, so this is not a controlled comparison. On this suite the completeness the manual says the setting may
  1882	cost was not observed to be lost.
  1883	
  1884	**F-10 — the degraded companions fail on link 1a or link 2b, queries outside every failing companion's registered failed set** *(added 2026-10-01)*.
  1885	Eleven of twenty-four case-(d) companion runs ended without a result. Six stalled on link 1a, the first
  1886	query, and five on link 2b's one-signer or slot-A conjunct (*The degraded wave*). The degraded correct
  1887	form itself lands in 6 388 s. The diagnosis (`dev/abl_d/REPORT.md`) explains the first six by the
  1888	product of attacker-mintable tuples in (d) and a mutation that unbinds a key. It does not explain three
  1889	of the later five. This is the registration's own mechanism-viability outcome, and §3 has no disposal
  1890	for it. **It is not a property result**, and nothing in this file reads it as one.
  1891	
  1892	---
  1893	

