  1513	## Recuts (encoding, not property)
  1514	
  1515	On the S-P3 pattern (`s-p3/RESULTS.md:38-73`). Each of the following is a way of **saying the same
  1516	thing** so that the model type-checks or terminates. **None of them changes a property, a query's
  1517	shape, or a registered prediction.** Where the registration was silent, the house pattern of the
  1518	sibling families was followed and is named.
  1519	
  1520	**R-1 — the wrapper's inner blob carries the inner OBJECT's signer slots.** S-P7's embedding is
  1521	`wrap(cvInner, (fbI, sgI))` — one inner frame and one inner signature
  1522	(`sp7_q2_degraded_compromised.pv:317-334`). The capstone's inner layer performs **S-P2's set logic**,
  1523	so a wrapped inner object whose manifest is the two-signer `M2` has **two** frames and **two**
  1524	signatures. The constructor is unchanged, `wrap(bitstring, bitstring)`; what widens is the opaque
  1525	blob, from `(fbI, sgI)` to `(fbIA, sgIA, fbIB, sgIB)`. Encoding only: the blob is opaque to every
  1526	check exactly as before, the type equalities of §3 C-Q6 (3) are untouched, and no query moves.
  1527	
  1528	**R-2 — the layer pipeline.** ProVerif has no recursion, and the families hand-write each depth
  1529	(S-P7's `VerifierWrapped` / `VerifierWrapped2`). The capstone instead carries the layer index on a
  1530	private channel `stageCh`: the front end emits the outermost object at `L0`, `WrapStage(L0, L1)` and
  1531	`WrapStage(L1, L2)` each accept one wrapper layer and emit the layer inside it, and `AttestStage`
  1532	accepts an attestation layer at whatever index it is handed. **Having exactly two `WrapStage`
  1533	instances is what bounds the depth at 2** (L-13's named scoping), exactly as S-P7's two hand-written
  1534	paths do. `AttestStage` is shared by the base path and by the innermost layer of a wrapped
  1535	presentation, as S-P7's `InnerCheck` is shared by `VerifierBase` and `VerifierWrapped` — which is
  1536	why C-C4 and C-C5, registered as mutations "inside `InnerCheck`", are single edits here too.
  1537	
  1538	**R-3 — one `Rescoped`, not `RescopedD1` / `RescopedD2`.** §3 C-Q4 names `Rescoped` "at depths 1 and
  1539	2"; S-P7 splits it per depth because its two depths are two hand-written processes. Under R-2 one
  1540	judge covers both, and the layer index is in `LayerAccepted`, `ScopeReported` and `ChainBroken`. The
  1541	per-depth split of §3's producer column (`RescopedD1` / `RescopedD2`) is therefore a naming of S-P7's
  1542	models, not a query the capstone owes separately.
  1543	
  1544	**R-4 — `wctx` records the IMMEDIATELY ENCLOSING wrapper, not the outermost.** S-P7 depth 2 uses
  1545	`wctx2(kO, idO, kM, idM)`. Under R-2 the context handed to the layer inside is
  1546	`wctx(kThisLayer, idThisLayer, fbThisLayer)`, so at `L2` the context **is** the middle wrapper —
  1547	which is precisely what C-C15(ii)'s "scope taken from the middle layer rather than the innermost"
  1548	needs, and what C-C15(i) needs at `L1`. Depth is carried by the layer index. `HonestWrappedAccepted`
  1549	at depth 2 therefore names the middle wrapper rather than the outermost; it is a witness, and its
  1550	reachability is what is registered.
  1551	
  1552	**R-5 — link 3 and C-Q9 are one judge, and link 4 and C-Q8 are one judge.** §9 states it in terms:
  1553	"link 3's relation **IS** C-Q9's". The capstone fires `ChainBroken(lyr, LINK_3)` and
  1554	`PossessionUnbound(kX, t, ppf)` from the same failure of `checksign(ppf, kX) = (POSS, t)`, and
  1555	`ChainBroken(lyr, LINK_4)` and `SigBytesUnbound(kX, sg, fb)` from the same failure of
  1556	`checksign(sg, kX) = (BYTES, fb)`. Both remain **separate registered queries** with separate events
  1557	and separate results; what is shared is the report channel and the destructor's `else` branch — which
  1558	is what gives link 3 and link 4 the "including the case where it does not verify at all" clause §3
