   750	## Cross-check: every ladder-1 run against ladder 2
    39	## G1 (class C): authorship correspondences missing from C-C5's and C-C17's sets
    40	
    41	Both companions remove the binding that makes acceptance imply that the accepting key signed those bytes. C-C5 removes "signed bytes = presented bytes", and C-C17 removes "slot-B key = the key the tuple names". The authorship correspondence therefore falls by construction under the attack each row itself registers.
    42	
    43	C-C5's Cases cell states the attack (`PREDICTIONS.md:1774`): *"the issuer's honest wrapper signature, made under its honestly authorized key, is carried as the inner signature accompanying different, attacker-built attestation bytes"*. That is the trace, except that it takes skW (the separate honest wrapper) at L0 rather than an inner layer. L0 is in scope because the mutated AttestStage is S-P7's InnerCheck, which the base path shares. The family copy shares it the same way (`cap21d_sp7_strict_cc5_wrapped.pv:204-224`, `:211` `anyb`). So the fixture is not broken, and no excluded capability is used: skW and its Wrapper are §1.1 fixture, both channels' evidence for MW is honest, and nothing is enrolled.
    44	
    45	The descent declares no authorship query. `cap21d`/`cap21e` declare C-Q8's judge, four S-P7 queries and two witnesses, and nothing else; the row says so itself. For C-C17, `cap18e_sp2_strict_cc17.pv` emits `IssuerSigned` (`:298`) and `Accept2S` (`:355`) but declares no correspondence (results `:513-2086`).
    46	
    47	*(REPAIR 2026-10-01, skeptic S-6.)* That the descent declares no authorship query explains why the sets omit it; it does not make the red composition-only, and G1 does not rest on that. The S-P1 family itself shows authorship going red under byte unbinding: `d6e_sp1_q8all_samekey_unbound.out:809`, authorship `is false`, cited at `PREDICTIONS.md:1570` as *"C-C7(i)'s registered red"*. So the C-C5 red is a single-family consequence of the very binding C-C5 removes, which is why G1 is classed as a registration defect (a set omission, class C) and not as a finding.
    48	
    49	This is the defect class that §4's round-7 rule names (*"a family copy's declared queries are not the capstone's query set"*). The same omission was already repaired once, for C-C7, by skeptic read S-4 (`PREDICTIONS.md:1776`): *"It was in no set, which under §4's replacement rule makes the builder read a registered severing as a broken fixture."* It was never carried to C-C5 or C-C17.
    50	
    51	C-C5's honest-key red also contradicts the retained-query table (`:1570`). That table registers honest-key authorship green "all four" and names the composed exception only under C-C7(ii). C-C5's row calls its own attack *"the same-key-wrapping consequence C-C7(ii) already records, … now at the inner layer"*.
    52	
    53	C-C17's honest-key form stays green (`cap_cc17_a.out:27241`) because the attacker's slot-B key is not honest. The unrestricted form is the SignerForged severing seen through a quantifier over every acceptance.
    54	
    55	## G2 (class B): C-Q6 conjunct 1 (unrestricted) under the evidence-weakening companions
    56	
    57	Both C-C10 and C-C14 traces run entirely through the standing path: Front → SUnwrap → StandingPath → StandingDecide. No envelope event occurs. The standing path does its own check 1. This is registered: the C-Q6 mapping row at `PREDICTIONS.md:1018` says the `t = tI` join is enabled by *"the evidence checks — check 1 of §1.2 …; removed by C-C10 (and subverted by C-C14)"*, and C-C14's row says *"in both the authority-channel processes and the capstone's check 1"* (`:2502`). So `derive.py`'s global replace (`_drop_ev`, `cc14`) is the registered mutation, not an excess one.
    58	
    59	Once check 1 no longer pins the tuple, any attacker can present a tuple that names its own key. It can sign its own TLR (`fp(kT) = kfpr`, `kT = kX`), forge an `anchorProof`, and obtain ESTABLISHED for a key no honest process designated. The honest-key forms stay green in all four runs (`cap_cc10_a.out:11697`, `:11894`; `cap_cc14_a.out:38010`, `:38666`; and the same in (b)). So this is not a severing of D12. It is the standing-path analogue of the unrestricted `Accept ⟹ IssuerSigned`, which both companions already register red.
    60	
    61	The dependence is shown without any mutation by case (c). With both channels leaked, `cap_cc01_c.out:12611` is red while `:12847` and `:13083` stay green, which is the unstated (c) red B-4 records. *(REPAIR 2026-10-01, skeptic S-6: C-Q6 is not stated for (c) and RESULTS.md reads that line nowhere; its use here is **diagnostic only**, a trace read to understand G2, not a registered reading. G2's classification does not depend on it.)* The isolation control `cap_cc10iso_a.out:5035` is green, because the honest channel's check is retained.
    62	
    63	The single-family descents could not exhibit this. C-C10 descends from S-P1 strict copies (`cap17e`, `cap20g`), and C-C14 from the first-link spike, which declares exactly two queries. Neither has a standing path. C-C10's set (2) predicted C-Q6 green *"on the same descent"* (`:1779`), and C-C14's set (2) does not name C-Q6.
    64	
    65	This is the consequence of composition that §4 reserves for a finding. The standing verdict's unrestricted form rests on authority evidence exactly as `Accept ⟹ IssuerSigned` does, so it inherits that query's §1.1 dependence (`:1570`: *"The (a)/(b) green depends on §1.1's reading of 'adversary-enrolled'"*).
    66	
    67	*(REPAIR 2026-10-01, skeptic S-6. The paragraphs above say the families could not exhibit this, which is right; read as "only the composition could show it", they overstate, because the **registration** already encodes the dependence. The C-Q6 mapping row names check 1 as the check that pins the standing path's `t`, "removed by **C-C10** (and subverted by **C-C14**)" (`PREDICTIONS.md:1018`), and the unrestricted form is stated for strict cases only. The corrected statement:)* **the composition confirms a dependence the registration encoded but mis-predicted in C-C10's set (2)** (`:1779`, green "on the same descent", a descent with no standing path). The amendment-shaped route this leaves — C-Q6 conjunct 1 unrestricted into C-C10's set (1) for (a)/(b), and into C-C14's — is RESULTS.md's amendment-shaped item 5, a clerk finding not entered.
    68	
    69	## G3 (class A): C-C15(i)'s transcription reports the presented inner key, not the wrapper key
    70	
    71	`derive.py:cc15i` (`:605-632`) builds AttestStageW. It verifies the inner signature under kEncW, and re-keys only the scope reports (`ATTRIB_BOTH`) and `sigCh`. LayerAccepted, Accept, link2aCh, possCh, bytesCh, link5Ch, slotCh, setCh, acceptCh, acceptKeyCh, gsigCh and chainCh still carry kA or kB. After the mutation those are presented keys that no check touches (`diff capstone.pv cap_cc15i_a.pv`, `+682-771`).
    72	
    73	The registration states the report contract the other way. It says *"the inner acceptance of this configuration reports the **presented inner** `(kW, tI, ppfI)`"* (`PREDICTIONS.md:2503`, the Cases cell). Its registered strict SignerForged route is the slot *"reported as satisfied by the **wrapper** key"*. The family descents report kW everywhere: `cap25a_sp7_strict_q6b_sigjudges.pv:331-336` has `AcceptInner(…, kW, …)`, `acceptCh`/`Report`/`pqCh`/`genericAccepted` with kW. That is where C-Q7 and C-Q8 were descended green (`cap25a:1306`, `:1317`).
    74	
    75	Every G3 trace turns on exactly this: the inner signature verifies under pk(skI), or `pk(a_3)` in (d), while the judge sees an unrelated presented key. The derived file therefore mutates more than the registered severing.
    76	
    77	Confirmation run. In `dv_cc15i_kw_{a,b,d}`, kEncW replaces kA/kB in AttestStageW's events and reports only, never in a check (the diff is in `mk_cc15i_kw.py`).
    78	
    79	- **(a):** C-Q7 `:4908`, C-Q8 `:4980`, link 4 `:6164`, C-Q3 `:7784`/`:7856`, `Accept ⟹ IssuerSigned` `:10016` and honest-key authorship `:10114` all go GREEN. Every registered strict red still fires: link 1a `:3331`, link 1b `:3879`, link 2a `:4434`, C-Q9 `:5535`, link 3 `:6092`, link 5 `:6719`, C-Q5 `:8561` and SignerForged `:10745`. Every witness is still reachable (`:11410-16676`).
    80	- **(b):** identical RESULT sets (C-Q7 `:4919`, C-Q8 `:4992`, link 4 `:6179`, C-Q3 `:7804`/`:7877`, Accept `:10049`, honest-key `:10148`, all green).
    81	- **(d), trimmed copy:** C-Q8 `:3595`, link 4 `:3843` and honest-key authorship `:6367` go GREEN. C-Q7 `:3348` and C-Q3 `:4505`/`:5206` stay red, which (d) registers. C-Q7 takes the registered D-4 `dsks` route on a wrapper key minted on the sole channel. `AcceptS` `:5959` stays red as the (d) baseline. The witnesses are reachable at `:7033`, `:7636` and `:8381`.
    82	
    83	## G4 (class C): C-Q2 under C-C15(i) in strict
    84	
    85	C-Q2 stays red in the repaired copies (`dv_cc15i_kw_a.out:7712`, `dv_cc15i_kw_b.out:7731`), with the same trace: one unchecked evidence pair and two attacker tuples. So it is a consequence of the registered mutation and not of G3.
    86	
    87	The registration's own sentence entails it: *"this mutation checks none of [the presented inner terms] … Every registered relation over the reported inner terms is therefore exposed"* (`:2503`). C-Q2 (D9's pair judge over `(evD, evR, t)`) is such a relation. Yet it is in neither set (1) nor set (2) for (i). No descent copy (`cap22e`–`cap25b`, `cap14b`) declares a C-Q2 judge, which is the round-7 class again. C-Q2 is not stated for (d), so this item exists in (a)/(b) only.
    88	
    89	## C-C15(i) and conjunct 3: the registration contradicts itself in strict (class C; not a divergence)
    90	
    91	Set (1) says both configurations red C-Q6 conjunct 3, with (i) reporting `(kW, idW) ≠ (kI, idI)` (`:2503`). The same row's strict Cases cell says *"the key half [is severed] by nothing the strict fixture can present, so D11's discharge in (a)/(b) rests on (iii)"*. The runs agree with the Cases cell: conjunct 3 is green in `cap_cc15i_a.out:12469` and `cap_cc15i_b.out:12508` (and in both repaired copies), and red in (d) at `cap_cc15i_d.out:20377`.
    92	
    93	Why the strict fixture forces green (a reading of the model, not a run): AttestStageW accepts the inner signature only under kEncW. The standing path pins `sg = sgI` by term equality inside a core whose lineage the TLR signs under `kT = kX`, with `fp(kT) = kfpr` of a tuple both channels evidence. In strict that is an honest key, so `kEncW = kX`. One key holds one manifest (skI ↔ M2/issuerId2), so the wrapper's identity equals the standing path's.
    94	
    95	Set (1)'s conjunct-3 membership for (i) needs case assignment to (d) only. The assembler was right to leave it open rather than score it.
    96	
    97	## What each class implies for the record
    98	
    99	- **A (G3; C-C15(i), 17 RESULT lines in (a), (b) and (d)): repair the fixture and re-run.** In `derive.py:cc15i`, report kEncW in place of kA/kB in AttestStageW's events and reports, exactly as `mk_cc15i_kw.py` does, keeping the scope reports as they are. Regenerate `cap_cc15i_{a,b,d}.pv` and re-run all three. The repaired copies predict that run: about 4 min each for (a)/(b), and (d) took 7468 s in the ladder. Record the broken fixture and its repair in RESULTS.md, as `cap_cc03_a` was. The diagnostic copies are evidence, not ladder results.
   100	- **B (G2; C-C10 (a)/(b), C-C14 (a)/(b)): a finding for the reviewer and the author.** C-Q6 conjunct 1's unrestricted form is red whenever authority evidence can be had for an attacker-key tuple. That is the standing-path twin of `Accept ⟹ IssuerSigned`, and case (c) shows it unmutated. It reaches C-C10's set (2) as a registered green gone red. *(REPAIR 2026-10-01, skeptic S-6: the case-(c) clause is diagnostic only; the finding is a dependence the registration encoded at `PREDICTIONS.md:1018` and mis-predicted in C-C10's set (2); and it now has an amendment-shaped route, RESULTS.md item 5.)*

