  1605	*What the setting does.* ProVerif saturates the model's Horn clauses by resolution. In each clause the
  1606	**selection function** picks the hypothesis that resolution unifies on. ProVerif 2.05's default is
  1607	`TermMaxsize`. `Term` gives every ordinary hypothesis weight 0 instead of its size, which removes the
  1608	preference for large facts (manual extract :8040 and :8047-8050, recorded in
  1609	`dev/abl2a/manual_notes.md:20-23`). The setting changes which clauses are derived on the way to the
  1610	fixpoint and how many. It changes neither the clauses the model generates nor what a query asks.
  1611	
  1612	*The soundness argument.* The ProVerif 2.05 manual, §6.6, verbatim: *"The performance settings may
  1613	result in more or fewer false attacks, but they never sacrifice soundness. It follows that when ProVerif
  1614	says that a property is satisfied, then the model really does guarantee that property, regardless of how
  1615	ProVerif has been configured using the settings presented here."* The quotation was taken from the
  1616	manual PDF on 2026-09-20 and is recorded in `dev/abl2a/manual_notes.md:6-11`. **Consequence:** under
  1617	`Term` an `is true` stays correct. The setting can cost only completeness: a false attack (`cannot be
  1618	proved`) or a slower run.
  1619	
  1620	*Why uniformly.* Applied only to the runs that died, the setting would build the tables under two
  1621	configurations (`dev/abl2a/REPORT.md:381-384`). Applied to all 80 files, every companion still differs
  1622	from its correct form by its mutation alone. The alternative was ablation step 1, which deletes the
  1623	depth-2 layer this model exists to compose. It was held in reserve for whatever still failed.
